Not in that example. "Me" refers to an indentity, this identity is not
different because it(s attributes) changed from 1987 to 1995. If I had
said "this is who I was in 1987", you would notice that this implies a
different identity - not "me", but as if to say "someone else".

I can't imagine a use of "me" or "I" that is ambiguous.

